          %h3#quantization Quantization
          %p
            Despite its fundamental role in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of Yang-Mills theory are still open. See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
          %h2#applications Applications
          %p
            All gauge fields in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            as well as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} GUT
            models are Yang–Mills fields.
          %p The matter fields in the standard model are spinors charged under the Yang-Mills field. See
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors+in+Yang-Mills+theory"} spinors in Yang-Mills theory
          %h2#History History
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#JaffeWitten"} Jaffe-Witten
          %blockquote
            %p
              By the 1950s, when Yang–Mills theory was discovered, it was already known that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum version
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} Maxwell theory
              – known as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} Quantum Electrodynamics
              or QED – gives an extremely accurate account of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+fields"} electromagnetic fields
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} forces
            %p
              So it was natural to inquire whether
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abelian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
              described other
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forces"} forces
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} nature
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+nuclear+force"} weak force
              (responsible among other things for certain forms of radioactivity) and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong or nuclear force
              (responsible among other things for the binding of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/protons"} protons
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrons"} neutrons
              into nuclei). The massless nature of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
              Yang–Mills waves was a serious obstacle to applying Yang–Mills theory to the other forces, for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+nuclear+force"} weak
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} nuclear forces
              are short range and many of the particles are massive. Hence these phenomena did not appear to be associated with long-range fields describing massless particles.
            %p
              In the 1960s and 1970s, physicists overcame these obstacles to the physical interpretation of non-abelian gauge theory. In the case of the weak force, this was accomplished by the Glashow–Salam–Weinberg
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+theory"} electroweak theory
              with gauge group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H =
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ×
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} U(1)
              = succeed "\u201D," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
              Yang–Mills waves. The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
              transforms in a two-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
              of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
              reduces the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+group"} structure group
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              to a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonally
              embedded in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SU
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2) \times U(1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
              describes both the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetic
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+nuclear+force"} weak forces
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
              only, in accord with what we see in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} nature
            %p
              The solution to the problem of massless Yang–Mills fields for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong interactions
              has a completely different nature. That solution did not come from adding fields to Yang–Mills theory, but by discovering a remarkable property of the quantum Yang–Mills theory itself, that is, of the quantum theory whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian
              has been given
              = succeed "above" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              = succeed "\u201D." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+freedom"} asymptotic freedom
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum
              behavior very similar to its classical behavior; yet at long distances the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical theory
              is no longer a good guide to the quantum behavior of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+freedom"}> Asymptotic freedom
              , together with other experimental and theoretical discoveries made in the 1960s and 1970s, made it possible to describe the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} nuclear force
              by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abelian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
              in which the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
              is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G =
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(3)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
              describe, at the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical level
              = succeed ",\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spin 1/2 objects
              somewhat analogous to the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+representation"} fundamental representation
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SU
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(3)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} Quantum Chromodynamics
              (QCD).
            %p
              The use of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              to describe the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong force
              was motivated by a whole series of experimental and theoretical discoveries made in the 1960s and 1970s, involving the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetries"} symmetries
              and high-energy behavior of the strong interactions. But
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abelian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
              is very different from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observed world
              of
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong interactions
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              to describe the strong force successfully, it must have at the quantum level the following three properties, each of which is dramatically different from the behavior of the classical theory:
            %p
              (1) It must have a “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"}> mass gap
              ;” namely there must be some constant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \gt 0
              such that every excitation of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
              at least
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            %p
              (2) It must have “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
              = succeed ",\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(3)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proton"} proton
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutron"} neutron
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pion"} pion
              –are
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(3)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
            %p
              (3) It must have “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+symmetry+breaking"}> chiral symmetry breaking
              ,” which means that the vacuum is potentially invariant (in the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
              of the full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
              that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acts
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %p
              The first point is necessary to explain why the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} nuclear force
              is strong but short-ranged; the second is needed to explain why we never see individual
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+algebra"} current algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pions"} pions
              that was developed in the 1960s.
            %p
              Both
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
              – since
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              has numerous successes in confrontation with experiment – and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} computer simulations
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
          %p
            This is the problem of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+law"} Gauss law
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D5+Yang-Mills+theory"} D=5 Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D2+Yang-Mills+theory"} D=2 Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/massive+Yang-Mills+theory"} massive Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+Yang-Mills+theory"} self-dual Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tr%28%CF%95%C2%B3%29"} Tr(ϕ³)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+coupling"} minimal coupling
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+double+line+notation"} 't Hooft double line notation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Maxwell+theory"} Einstein-Maxwell theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Maxwell-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"} Einstein-Maxwell-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+equation"} Yang-Mills equation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors+in+Yang-Mills+theory"} spinors in Yang-Mills theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"}> QED
                    ,
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+field"} electroweak field
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang+monopole"}> Yang monopole
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft-Polyakov+monopole"} 't Hooft-Polyakov monopole
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"}> S-duality
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Montonen-Olive+duality"} Montonen-Olive duality
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric-magnetic+duality"} electric-magnetic duality
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+duality"} geometric Langlands duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proca+theory"} Proca theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instanton"} Yang-Mills instanton
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+freedom"} asymptotic freedom
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uncertainty+of+fluxes"} uncertainty of fluxes
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Yang-Mills theory is named after the article
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen+Ning+Yang"}> Chen Ning Yang
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Mills"} Robert Mills
              = succeed "." do
                %em Conservation of Isotopic Spin and Isotopic Gauge Invariance
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://prola.aps.org/abstract/PR/v96/i1/p191_1"} web
          %p
            which was the first to generalize the principle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+group"} non-abalian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+interactions"} weak interactions
            (only) after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneous+symmetry+breaking"} spontaneous symmetry breaking
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+mechanism"} Higgs mechanism
          %p
            The identification of Yang-Mills
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections"} connections
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
            is due to:
          %ul
            %li#WuYang75
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tai+Tsun+Wu"}> Tai Tsun Wu
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen+Ning+Yang"} Chen Ning Yang
              = succeed "," do
                %em Concept of nonintegrable phase factors and global formulation of gauge fields
              %strong 12
              (1975) 3845 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.12.3845"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.12.3845
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p On the historical origins:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A. C. T. Wu,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen+Ning+Yang"} Chen Ning Yang
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Evolution of the concept of vector potential in the description of the fundamental interactions
                %strong 21
                16 (2006) 3235-3277 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217751X06033143"}> doi:10.1142/S0217751X06033143
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen+Ning+Yang"}> Chen Ning Yang
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The conceptual origins of Maxwell’s equations and gauge theory
                %strong 67
                11 (2014) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/PT.3.2585"}> doi:10.1063/PT.3.2585
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://home.ustc.edu.cn/~lxsphys/2021-3-18/The%20conceptual%20origins%20of%20Maxwell%27s%20equations%20and%20gauge%20theory.pdf"} pdf
          %p Review of the basics:
          %ul
            %li#JaffeWitten00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Jaffe"}> Arthur Jaffe
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
                %em Quantum Yang-Mills theory
                (2000) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.claymath.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/yangmills.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.arthurjaffe.com/Assets/pdf/QuantumYangMillsWebRevised.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/JaffeWitten-QuantumYangMills.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (in the context of the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap+problem"} mass gap problem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikio+Nakahara"}> Mikio Nakahara
                , Section 10.5.4 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry%2C+Topology+and+Physics"} Geometry, Topology and Physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1201/9781315275826"} doi:10.1201/9781315275826
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://alpha.sinp.msu.ru/~panov/LibBooks/GRAV/(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)Mikio_Nakahara-Geometry,_Topology_and_Physics,_Second_Edition_(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)-Institute_of_Physics_Publishing(2003).pdf"} pdf
            %li#Donaldson05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Donaldson"}> Simon Donaldson
                ,
                %em Yang-Mills theory and geometry
                (2005) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.ma.imperial.ac.uk/~skdona/YMILLS.PDF"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Donaldson-YangMillsGeometry.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Donaldson06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Donaldson"}> Simon Donaldson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gauge Theory: Mathematical Applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/B0-12-512666-2/00075-4"} doi:10.1016/B0-12-512666-2/00075-4
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://wwwf.imperial.ac.uk/~skdona/EMP.PDF"} author pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/DonaldsonGaugeTheory.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jos%C3%A9+Figueroa-O%27Farrill"}> José Figueroa-O'Farrill
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gauge theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://empg.maths.ed.ac.uk/Activities/GT/index.html"} web
            %li#Uhlenbeck12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karen+Uhlenbeck"}> Karen Uhlenbeck
                , notes by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laura+Fredrickson"} Laura Fredrickson
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Equations of Gauge Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://web.stanford.edu/~ljfred4/Attachments/TempleLectures.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/UhlenbeckGaugeTheory.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerd+Rudolph"}> Gerd Rudolph
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Schmidt"} Matthias Schmidt
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Differential Geometry and Mathematical Physics Part II. Fibre Bundles, Topology and Gauge Fields
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-94-024-0959-8"} doi:10.1007/978-94-024-0959-8
          %p
            See also the references at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+monopole"} Yang-Mills monopole
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instanton"} Yang-Mills instanton
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            Classical discussion of YM-theory over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
            (which is closely related to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+flat+connections"} moduli space of flat connections
          %ul
            %li#AtiyahBott83
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raoul+Bott"} Raoul Bott
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Yang-Mills equations over Riemann surfaces
              %p
                Vol. 308, No. 1505 (Mar. 17, 1983), pp. 523-615 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/37156"}> jstor
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.stackexchange.com/a/295505/58526"} lighning summary
          %p which is reviewed in the lecture notes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Evans"}> Jonathan Evans
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Aspects of Yang-Mills theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.homepages.ucl.ac.uk/~ucahjde/yangmills.htm"} web
          %p
            Relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+Floer+homology"} instanton Floer homology
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Donaldson"}> Simon Donaldson
              ,
              %em Floer homology groups in Yang-Mills theory
              Cambridge University Press (2002) (
              %a{:href => "http://catdir.loc.gov/catdir/samples/cam031/2001035888.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tamagawa+numbers"} Tamagawa numbers
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aravind+Asok"}> Aravind Asok
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brent+Doran"} Brent Doran
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frances+Kirwan"} Frances Kirwan
              = succeed "," do
                %em Yang-Mills theory and Tamagawa numbers: the fascination of unexpected links in mathematics.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/arXiv:0801.4733"} arXiv:0801.4733
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
            of YM
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nima+Arkani-Hamed"}> Nima Arkani-Hamed
              , Qu Cao, Jin Dong, Carolina Figueiredo, Song He:
              %em Scalar-Scaffolded Gluons and the Combinatorial Origins of Yang-Mills Theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.00041"}> arXiv:2401.00041
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonperturbative+effect"} nonperturbative effect
            of “effective locality”:
          %ul
            %li
              Yves Gabellini, Thierry Grandou, Ralf Hofmann:
              %em On the Yang-Mills propagator at strong coupling
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.12124"}> arXiv:2412.12124
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#classical_solutions Classical solutions
          %p
            Wu and Yang (1968) found a static solution to the sourceless
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+equations"} Yang-Mills equations
          %ul
            %li
              J. A. O. Marinho, O. Oliveira, B. V. Carlson, T. Frederico,
              %em Revisiting the Wu-Yang Monopole: classical solutions and conformal invariance
          %p There is an old review,
          %ul
            %li
              Alfred Actor,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Classical solutions of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi SU
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
                  Yang—Mills theories
          %p
            that provides some of the known solutions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            gauge theory in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monopoles"}> monopoles
            , plane waves, etc) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            and their cousins). For general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+groups"} gauge groups
            one can get solutions by embedding
            = succeed "\u2018s." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instantons"} Yang-Mills instantons
            the most general solution is known, first worked out by
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nigel+Hitchin"} Nigel Hitchin
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Drinfeld"} Vladimir Drinfeld
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Manin"} Yuri Manin
              = succeed "," do
                %em Construction of instantons
              %a{:href => "http://www.new.ox.ac.uk/system/files/ADHM.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+groups"} classical groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} SO
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+group"} Sp
          %ul
            %li
              C. Bernard, N. Christ, A. Guth, E. Weinberg,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Pseudoparticle Parameters for Arbitrary Gauge Groups
              = succeed "," do
                %strong D16
          %p
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+groups"} exceptional Lie groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instanton"} Yang-Mills instanton
            story is the construction of solutions with non-trivial
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
          %ul
            %li
              Thomas C. Kraan, Pierre van Baal,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Periodic instantons with nontrivial holonomy
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9805168"} hep-th/9805168
          %p There is a nice set of lecture notes
          %ul
            %li
              David Tong,
              %em TASI Lectures on Solitons
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0509216"}> hep-th/0509216
              ),
          %p
            on topological solutions with different co-dimension (instantons, monopoles, vortices, domain walls). Note, however, that except for instantons these solutions typically require extra scalars and broken U(1)‘s, as one may find in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theories"} super Yang-Mills theories
          %p Some of the material used here has been taken from
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://physics.stackexchange.com/"}> P.SE
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/27309/which-exact-solutions-of-the-classical-yang-mills-equations-are-known"} Which exact solutions of the classical Yang-Mills equations are known?
          %p
            Another model featuring Yang-Mills fields has been proposed by Curci and Ferrari, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Curci-Ferrari+model"} Curci-Ferrari model
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              DispersiveWiki,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://wiki.math.toronto.edu/DispersiveWiki/index.php/Yang-Mills_equations"} Yang-Mills equations
          %h3#ReferencesPhaseSpaceAndCanonicalQuantization Phase space and canonical quantization
          %p
            On the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+brackets"} Poisson brackets
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            in Yang-Mills theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Taichiro Kugo, Izumi Ojima,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Manifestly Covariant Canonical Formulation of the Yang-Mills Field Theories. I: General Formalism
                %strong 60
                6 (1978) 1869–1889 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1143/PTP.60.1869"}> doi:10.1143/PTP.60.1869
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#FriedmanPapastamatiou83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+L.+Friedman"}> John L. Friedman
                , Nicholas J. Papastamatiou,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the canonical quantization of Yang-Mills theories
                %strong 219
                1 (1983) 125-142 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(83)90431-5"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(83)90431-5
                ]
            %li#BassettoLazzizzeraSoldati84
              %p
                A. Bassetto, I. Lazzizzera, R. Soldati,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Yang-Mills theories in space-like axial and planar gauges
                %strong 236
                2 (1984) 319-335 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(84)90538-8"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(84)90538-8
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                D. M. Gitman, S. L. Lyakhovich &amp; I. V. Tyutin,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Canonical quantization of the Yang-Mills Lagrangian with higher derivatives
                %strong 28
                (1985) 554–556 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00896182"}> doi:10.1007/BF00896182
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Kurt Haller,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Yang-Mills theory and quantum chromodynamics in the temporal gauge
                %strong 36
                (1987) 1839 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.36.1839"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.36.1839
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Haagensen93
              %p
                P. E. Haagensen,
                %em On The Exact Implementation Of Gauss’ Law In Yang-Mills Theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9307319"}> arXiv:hep-ph/9307319
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sarada+G.+Rajeev"}> Sarada G. Rajeev
                , O. T. Turgut,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Poisson Algebra of Wilson Loops in Four-Dimensional Yang-Mills Theory
                %strong 10
                (1995) 2479 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9410053"}> arXiv:hep-th/9410053
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217751X95001194"} doi:10.1142/S0217751X95001194
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (in
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light-front+formalism"} light-front formalism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Dimock"}> Jonathan Dimock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Canonical Quantization of Yang-Millson a circle
                %strong 08
                01 (1996) 85-102 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0129055X96000044"}> doi:10.1142/S0129055X96000044
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#BlaschkeGieres21
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+N.+Blaschke"}> Daniel N. Blaschke
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fran%C3%A7ois+Gieres"} François Gieres
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the canonical formulation of gauge field theories and Poincaré transformations
                %strong 965
                (2021) 115366 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2021.115366"}> doi:10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2021.115366
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.14406"} arXiv:2004.14406
            %li#Riello21
              %p
                Aldo Riello,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symplectic reduction of Yang-Mills theory with boundaries: from superselection sectors to edge modes, and back
                %strong 10
                125 (2021) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://scipost.org/SciPostPhys.10.6.125"}> doi:10.21468/SciPostPhys.10.6.125
                \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
